<h2 class="p1">Neural Engine and Media Capabilities</h2>
<h4 class="p1">Both chips include:</h4>
<ul>
<li class="p1">16-core Neural Engine with higher memory bandwidth connection</li>
<li class="p1">Hardware-accelerated H.264 and HEVC</li>
<li class="p1">AV1 decode support</li>
<li class="p1">ProRes encode and decode engines</li>
<li class="p1">Memory Integrity Enforcement</li>
<li class="p1">Integrated Thunderbolt 5 controllers</li>
</ul>
<p class="p1">AI acceleration is strong across both models, but M5 Max benefits from higher GPU-based Neural Accelerator throughput and expanded memory bandwidth.</p>
<h2 class="p1">Thunderbolt 5 and Connectivity</h2>
<p class="p1">Each Thunderbolt 5 port is supported by its own controller integrated directly on the SoC. Both M5 Pro and M5 Max support Thunderbolt 5, enabling high-speed external storage, multiple high-resolution displays, and advanced workstation setups.</p>
<p class="p1">The difference lies not in connectivity capability, but in how much GPU power and memory bandwidth can be allocated to external display workflows and real-time rendering tasks.</p>
<p class="p1">Which Chip Is Built for Which Workflow?</p>
<h4 class="p1">M5 Pro is designed for:</h4>
<ul>
<li class="p1">Developers compiling large codebases</li>
<li class="p1">Engineers running data models</li>
<li class="p1">Creative professionals editing 4K content</li>
<li class="p1">Advanced productivity users</li>
</ul>
<h4 class="p1">M5 Max targets:</h4>
<ul>
<li class="p1">3D animators</li>
<li class="p1">AI researchers training large models</li>
<li class="p1">Visual effects artists</li>
<li class="p1">Professionals working with 8K multi-stream timelines</li>
<li class="p1">Simulation-heavy scientific workloads</li>
</ul>
<p class="p1">Both chips push Apple silicon forward with Fusion Architecture, expanded AI compute, and next-generation GPU technology. The decision between M5 Pro vs M5 Max ultimately comes down to GPU scale and memory bandwidth needs.</p>
